/* book exam traps: Treating δ as a real measured stagnant film. δ is an equivalent thickness chosen to match kc; it changes with flow and is not a property of the fluid. | A unit slip: δ in mm must be converted to m before D/δ. With D in m²/s, δ in m gives kc in m/s. | Using kc ∝ D for all data. Film theory doubles kc when D doubles; penetration and surface renewal increase it by only √2. | Omitting the factor 2 in penetration theory. The average flux over exposure time t has 2√(D/(πt)); dropping 2 halves the predicted kc. | Confusing surface renewal rate s with 1/t. In penetration theory t is a fixed exposure time; in surface renewal s is a random replacement rate. */
