/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - In turbulent flow, a fluid packet carries momentum, enthalpy and solute together.
   - The three fluxes are τ = ρ(ν + εM) du/dy, q/A = −ρ cp(α + εH) dT/dy and NA = −(DAB + εD) dCA/dy.
   - Two dimensionless groups compare molecular transport: Pr = ν/α = μ cp/k and Sc = ν/DAB = μ/(ρDAB).
   - St = h/(ρ cp u) is the Stanton number; h is the heat-transfer coefficient (W/(m²·K)); u is bulk velocity (m/s); f is the Fanning friction factor, defined by τw = f (1/2) ρ u², with τw the wall shear stress (Pa).
   - This is the Reynolds analogy.
   - Chilton and Colburn kept the same idea but absorbed the molecular-property effect into j-factors.
   - jH and jD are dimensionless; f is the Fanning friction factor; all other symbols are defined above.
   - The equality is used to estimate kc from a measured pressure drop.
   - For a flat plate, use the local or average skin-friction coefficient from boundary-layer tables in place of the pipe formula.
   - Prandtl-Taylor and von Kármán refined the analogy by keeping the laminar sublayer.
*/
