/* book Worked example: Q: Air at 298 K (ρ = 1.18 kg/m³, μ = 1.85×10⁻⁵ Pa·s) flows at 5.0 m/s through a smooth tube of diameter 50 mm whose inner wall is coated with naphthalene (DAB = 6.1×10⁻⁶ m²/s in air). The pressure drop over a straight 3.0 m length is 24.9 Pa. Use the Chilton–Colburn analogy to estimate kc, and compare with the Linton–Sherwood pipe correlation Sh = 0.023 Re0.83 Sc1/3.
   1. Re = ρ u d/μ = 1.18 × 5.0 × 0.050/1.85×10⁻⁵ = 1.59×10⁴: turbulent.
   2. Fanning factor from the pressure drop: f = Δp d/(2 L ρ u²) = 24.9 × 0.050/(2 × 3.0 × 1.18 × 25) = 7.03×10⁻³. (The Blasius value 0.079 Re−0.25 = 7.03×10⁻³ agrees, so the tube is smooth and the drop is all skin friction.)
   3. jD = f/2 = 3.52×10⁻³.
   4. ν = μ/ρ = 1.57×10⁻⁵ m²/s, so Sc = ν/DAB = 1.57×10⁻⁵/6.1×10⁻⁶ = 2.57 and Sc1/3 = 1.37.
   5. Sh = jD Re Sc1/3 = 3.52×10⁻³ × 1.59×10⁴ × 1.37 = 76.8, so kc = Sh DAB/d = 76.8 × 6.1×10⁻⁶/0.050 = 9.37×10⁻³ m/s.
   6. Linton–Sherwood: Sh = 0.023 × (1.59×10⁴)0.83 × 1.37 = 0.023 × 3078 × 1.37 = 97.0, kc = 1.18×10⁻² m/s.
   Answer: The analogy gives kc ≈ 9.4×10⁻³ m/s from a 25 Pa pressure reading; the direct correlation gives 1.2×10⁻² m/s. The two agree within about 20 %, which is the usual accuracy of either method. */
