/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Let y be the solute mole fraction in the bulk gas and x the solute mole fraction in the bulk liquid.
   - Each film carries flux with its own coefficient: NA = ky(y − yi) = kx(xi − x). ky is the gas-film coefficient and kx the liquid-film coefficient, both in kmol/(m²·s) per unit mole-fraction difference.
   - On the y-x plot the interface is where the equilibrium line meets a straight line from the bulk point (x, y).
   - NA is the solute flux in kmol/(m²·s); ky is the gas-film coefficient in kmol/(m²·s); kx is the liquid-film coefficient in kmol/(m²·s); y and x are bulk mole fractions; yi and xi are interface mole fractions.
   - Designers prefer one driving force, not two.
   - Write NA = Ky(y − y*) and NA = Kx(x* − x).
   - Ky is the overall gas-phase coefficient in kmol/(m²·s); ky and kx are the individual film coefficients; m is the slope of the equilibrium line y = m x.
   - Kx is the overall liquid-phase coefficient in kmol/(m²·s); the other symbols are as above.
   - The factor m appears because kx uses Δx while Ky uses Δy.
   - Read the shares before designing.
*/
