/* book exam traps: Assuming the bulk gas and bulk liquid are in equilibrium. Equilibrium holds only at the interface; y* = m x is a hypothetical gas in equilibrium with the bulk liquid, not the actual bulk gas. | Mixing interface and bulk values: using y* = m xi instead of y* = m x, or yi = m x instead of yi = m xi. | Adding ky and kx directly. They use different driving forces, so the m factor must appear: 1/Ky = 1/ky + m/kx, not 1/ky + 1/kx. | Placing the interface point off the equilibrium line. It lies on the equilibrium line, exactly where the line of slope −kx/ky drawn from the bulk point meets it. | Forgetting units: ky and kx are in kmol/(m²·s) per unit mole-fraction difference, and mole fractions have no unit. Check that each NA comes out in kmol/(m²·s). */
